 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Perth's electricity prices are rising far more slowly than the national average, so the pressure on your bill probably isn't coming from the power tariff itself.**\n\n**Electricity prices, annual change in the ABS CPI (August 2026)**\n- Perth: +2.4%.\n- National (eight-capital average): +13.2%.\n\nPerth's price has gone up about 2.4–2.5% a year in every recent month outside the rebate-affected ones. It rose from 100 to 102.44 between July 2025 and July 2026, which looks like a modest annual tariff adjustment. Nationally, the electricity index went from about 94 in mid-2025 to about 114 in 2026.\n\n**Why Perth's numbers look odd**\nThe Perth electricity series swings wildly. For example, it showed −50% in late 2024 and +289% in August 2025. I think that comes from government energy rebate credits being applied and then dropping out of the index. It isn't a real tripling of prices, so don't read those spikes as actual price moves. The steady 2.4–2.5% months are the cleaner signal.\n\n**Why your bill may still feel huge**\n- **Overall cost of living is up.** Perth's all-groups CPI was +4.3% in the year to August 2026, slightly above the national +4.0%. Everything is squeezing the budget at once.\n- **Rebates have faded.** Any one-off credits that cut your bills in earlier periods are no longer helping. That would make your bill look much bigger even though the underlying tariff has barely moved.\n- **Usage matters.** A hot summer with lots of air-conditioning, or an electric hot water or EV setup, can lift a bill a lot. The CPI measures the price per unit, not how much you use.\n\nTo pin down your own case, compare the kWh used and the daily supply charge on your last few bills. If the kWh are up, it's usage. If the rate per kWh is up more than about 2.5%, check whether you're on the right plan. Western Australia has a regulated tariff, so there's less room to shop around than in the eastern states.",
